<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willem Drees</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willem Drees was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Labour politician who served as Prime Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from 1948 to 1958, leading coalitions of social democrats and Catholics. His governments rebuilt the country after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, oversaw the end of Dutch rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and introduced a state old-age pension, which made him widely respected as the father of the Dutch welfare state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willem-drees.jpg" alt="Willem Drees" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksoverheid.nl · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_Drees_1958.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Willem Drees was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 5 July 1886 into an orthodox reformed middle-class family. His father, a banker, died when Drees was five, and he could continue his studies thanks to the support of his uncle Frits. He rejected the Christian creed at the age of eighteen. After hearing a speech by Pieter Jelles Troelstra in December 1902 he became a democratic socialist, and he joined the Social Democratic Workers' Party (SDAP) on his eighteenth birthday.</p><p>Drees chaired the party's branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> until 1931 and sat on the municipal council from 1913 to 1941. As alderman from 1919 he was responsible for social affairs, and after 1931 for finance and public works. He favoured a pragmatic, reformist course. Elected to the House of Representatives in 1933, he opposed the National Socialist Movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, saying in 1935 that fascists are not opponents but enemies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Drees succeeded Willem Albarda as party leader on 14 May 1940. During the German occupation he was taken hostage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a> on 7 October 1940, was moved to Sint-Michielsgestel in 1941 and was released on 11 May 1942 because of poor health. He then took a prominent part in the illegal executive committee of the SDAP. After the war he became Minister of Social Affairs and Deputy Prime Minister on 25 June 1945, and in February 1946 he was a co-founder of the Labour Party (PvdA) and its first leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the 1948 general election Drees formed a cabinet with the Catholic People's Party and became Prime Minister on 7 August 1948. He held the office until 22 December 1958 in four successive cabinets. His years in office saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>, economic reconstruction, the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> welfare state, and international integration, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. His cabinets also faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> flood of 1953. He advocated austerity and was known for thrift and simplicity: he went to work on foot or by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycle</a>.</p><p>Drees received the honorary title of Minister of State on 22 December 1958. He remained active as a historian and author. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 14 May 1988, at the age of 101.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willem Drees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics was dominated by confessional parties, including Abraham Kuyper’s Anti-Revolutionary Party, and since the Second World War by coalition governments of Christian democrats, social democrats and liberals, such as those of <b>Willem Drees</b> and, later, Ruud Lubbers, Wim Kok and Mark Rutte.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.37,4.88&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Amsterdam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Willem Drees"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 5641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 5641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.37,4.88&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
